","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-16T22:27:19Z","model":"gemini-4-argon-high","input_tokens":2321,"cache_read_tokens":51023,"output_tokens":850,"thinking":"Insertion sort compiled successfully. Now, investigating the imperative implementation and functional-imperative equivalence. Considering how to express this imperative version in Lean 4 with mutable state using `Id.run do`, then proving the equivalence.\n\n\nAnalyzing the Python loop to understand the algorithm's behavior, particularly the `inclusive` and `exclusive` methods. The key is in how `j` and `delta` are computed and used for interpolation. The imperative Lean 4 implementation using mutable state will mirror this structure. Focusing on the `i * m // n` calculation to map index `i` to `j`, ensuring correct index clamping.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"cd /home/user/lean_project && lake env lean --stdin << 'EOF'\nimport Mathlib\n\ndef mapImp {α β : Type*} (xs : List α) (f : α → β) : List β := Id.run do\n  let mut result : List β := []\n  for x in xs do\n    result := result ++ [f x]\n  return result\n\nlemma forIn_append_map {α β : Type*} (xs : List α) (f : α → β) (acc : List β) :\n    (ForIn.forIn xs acc (fun x r => pure (ForInStep.yield (r ++ [f x]))) : Id (List β)) =\n    acc ++ xs.map f := by\n  induction xs generalizing acc with\n  | nil => simp\n  | cons x xs ih =>\n    simp only [List.forIn_cons, pure_bind, ih, List.map_cons, List.append_assoc, List.singleton_append]\n\ntheorem mapImp_eq_map {α β : Type*} (xs : List α) (f : α → β) :\n    mapImp xs f = xs.map f := by\n  simpa [mapImp] using forIn_append_map xs f []\nEOF\n","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolSummary":"Test imperative loop equivalence proof in Lean 4"}}]}
